        "overview": "This lesson builds upon the previously established concepts of ʿadālah and ḍabṭ, moving from their definitions to their practical application within ḥadīth transmission. The focus shifts to the issue of riwāyah bi’l-maʿnā (narration by meaning), examining when preserving exact wording is obligatory and when conveying meaning may be permitted. Drawing heavily on the Ḥanafī methodological framework, the lesson explains the distinction between ʿazīmah and rukhsah in transmission, and clarifies why narrating verbatim remains superior despite conditional allowances. Through linguistic analysis, the scholars’ classification of wording—ranging from clear and unambiguous expressions to ambiguous and multi-meaning terms—is presented as the key to understanding these rulings.\n\nThe lesson further applies these principles to real evaluative scenarios, demonstrating how precision in language directly impacts the reliability of narrations. It discusses cases where narration by meaning is prohibited, such as in jawāmiʿ al-kalim, and explains how misinterpretation can result from altering legally or theologically sensitive wording. The discussion also extends to applied Jarḥ wa al-Taʿdīl, including the treatment of unknown narrators, methods of assessing ḍabṭ through comparison, and the ethical limits of criticism. By situating these discussions within Qurʾānic and Prophetic guidance, the lesson shows how the theoretical foundations laid earlier are operationalized to preserve both the accuracy of the Sunnah and the dignity of those who transmitted it.",
