        "portalId": "9af2d341-4978-4fbd-b635-4d596df9c87f",
        "title": "Lesson 7 | Conditions of Narrators Beyond ʿAdālah and Ḍabṭ",
        "overview": "This lesson examines whether the reliability of a narrator in ḥadīth transmission depends solely on the two foundational qualities of ʿadālah (moral uprightness) and ḍabṭ (precision), or whether additional conditions are required. It situates this inquiry within the broader development of ḥadīth methodology, especially the codification of the five conditions of ṣaḥīḥ ḥadīth by Ibn al-Ṣalāḥ and their later refinement by Ibn Ḥajar. The discussion clarifies that while ʿadālah and ḍabṭ form the essential core of narrator evaluation, other considerations—such as continuity of the chain, absence of shudhūdh, and hidden defects—function at the level of overall ḥadīth authenticity rather than personal trustworthiness. The lesson also corrects common misunderstandings, including the asymmetrical nature of Ibn al-Ṣalāḥ’s formulation and the mistaken attribution of restrictive conditions (such as masculinity) to early jurists like Abū Ḥanīfah.\n\nBeyond narrator integrity, the lesson explores how juristic methodology—particularly within the Ḥanafī tradition—interacts with ḥadīth criticism. It clarifies that concepts such as fiqh al-rāwī are not independent conditions of reliability but arise in the context of taʿāruḍ al-adillah (conflicting evidences) and tarjīḥ (weighing between proofs). Through discussions of shudhūdh, tafarrud, and the contributions of ʿĪsā ibn Abān, the lesson demonstrates that both muḥaddithūn and uṣūlīs operate upon shared rational principles, differing primarily in analytical scope rather than epistemic foundations. Ultimately, it presents the debate as one of methodological framing rather than substantive contradiction, highlighting the coherence and sophistication of classical Islamic scholarship.",
