        "overview": "This lesson continues the previous discussion on the conditions of narrator reliability in the science of al-Jarḥ wa al-Taʿdīl (الْجَرْحُ وَالتَّعْدِيلُ), moving beyond the two foundational requirements of ʿadālah (moral integrity) and ḍabṭ (precision). It examines additional conditions that some scholars proposed in specific contexts, particularly the consideration of fiqh (legal understanding) when a narrator transmits from memory or engages in riwāyah bi’l-maʿnā (transmission by meaning). The lesson clarifies that figures such as Ibn Ḥibbān did not establish fiqh as a universal requirement, but rather emphasized it in scenarios where the risk of altering meaning is greater. It also corrects misattributions related to Abū Ḥanīfah, affirming that the Ḥanafī school did not require narrators to be male and accepted female transmitters within the established methodological framework.\n\nThe lesson further analyzes other proposed conditions—such as fame in hearing ḥadīth, being sighted rather than blind, and having a known lineage—and demonstrates that these either reduce to the established principles of removing obscurity (jahālah) or lack sufficient independent justification. Through careful reasoning, it emphasizes that the science of ḥadīth criticism is built upon coherent logical principles aligned with the objectives of the Sharīʿah, rather than isolated textual proofs. Overall, the lesson reinforces that while ʿadālah and ḍabṭ remain the core criteria, additional scrutiny may be applied contextually when heightened caution is warranted, such as in cases of solitary transmission or potential interpretive distortion.",
