        "overview": "In this lesson, we cover the principles and applications of oaths in Islamic jurisprudence, beginning with the foundational guidelines that govern them. We emphasize the importance of understanding both the context and the intention behind an oath, as these elements play a central role in determining its meaning and legal implications. We examine various scenarios in which oaths may be taken, such as swearing not to enter a house or not to speak to a particular person, and explain how such oaths are interpreted according to linguistic usage and customary practice. We clarify that whether an oath is considered violated depends largely on its specific wording and on the customary understanding of terms like “house” or “speak,” which may differ from one setting to another.\n\nIn the latter part of the lesson, we address more detailed situations, including oaths related to refraining from wearing certain garments or consuming particular foods. We explain that the interpretation of such oaths often depends on the intention behind the statement and the common meanings associated with the words used. For example, an oath not to eat “meat” may or may not include fish, depending on prevailing custom. We also discuss the principle of allowing a reasonable amount of time for a person to fulfill an oath, underscoring that oaths are intended to be upheld rather than hastily deemed broken. Throughout the lesson, we draw upon examples from Islamic texts and scholarly discussions to demonstrate how these principles are applied in practical situations.",
