        "overview": "In this lesson, we examine the intricacies of oaths (aymān) in Islamic jurisprudence, with particular focus on the distinction between literal (ḥaqīqī) and metaphorical (majāzī) interpretations of language. We begin with the example of a person who swears not to eat wheat but later consumes bread made from that wheat. According to Imām Abū Ḥanīfah, priority is given to the literal meaning of the oath. Therefore, since the individual did not consume wheat in its raw form, the oath is not considered violated. We explore Abū Ḥanīfah’s principle that the literal meaning takes precedence as long as it remains linguistically valid, even if a metaphorical meaning is more commonly used in everyday speech. This approach is contrasted with other juristic opinions that give weight to customary usage and metaphorical understanding when it reflects common vernacular.\n\nIn this lesson, we also analyze several additional scenarios involving oaths, such as swearing not to speak to someone, not to enter a particular house, or not to eat certain foods. Each case is carefully examined to determine whether the oath has been broken, based on whether the words are interpreted literally or according to common usage. We emphasize the importance of context (siyāq), intention (niyyah), and custom (ʿurf) in interpreting legal statements. Furthermore, we discuss the role of saying “In shāʾ Allāh” in conjunction with an oath and how, when used properly, it may affect the binding nature of the oath. Overall, in this lesson we develop a structured understanding of how Islamic law approaches oaths, demonstrating the central role of linguistic analysis, intention, and social context in legal interpretation.",
