        "overview": "In this lesson, we begin by establishing the conceptual framework surrounding the notion of istiṭāʿa (ability) and the various meanings of power in Islamic theology and jurisprudence. We distinguish between two forms of ability: one referring to health, soundness of means, and absence of impediments, and another referring to an internal power that exists concurrently with the action itself. We analyze the phrase “if I can” through the lens of intention and customary usage, exploring how leading Ḥanafī scholars such as Abū Ḥanīfah, Abū Yūsuf, and Muḥammad differed regarding its theological and legal implications. Through statements like, “If one swears an oath that he shall definitely come to him, if he can,” we examine how intention, circumstance, and linguistic nuance determine whether an oath becomes binding. We also clarify the juristic distinction between true physical capacity and the practical enabling conditions of action—summarized in the principle that it refers to the “ability of health and not of capacity.”\n\nIn this lesson, we then move to practical rulings and applied case studies derived from these principles. We examine time-bound and indefinite oaths, explaining how default timeframes—such as six months—are assigned to unspecified terms like zaman or al-dahr, while noting differing scholarly opinions across the schools of law. We analyze scenarios such as swearing not to speak for an undefined period, not residing in a certain home, not eating specific meals, or not repaying a debt in installments, carefully identifying what constitutes a violation and when kaffārah (expiation) becomes due. Particular attention is given to nuanced cases such as paying a debt “dirham by dirham,” determining whether full payment or partial installments breach the oath. We also address oaths extending to major life events, death, and inheritance implications, including situations in which fulfillment becomes impossible. Throughout, we ground our discussion in classical fiqh reasoning and the linguistic precision of Arabic usage, illustrating how legal outcomes depend on intention, context, and established juristic principles.",
