        "overview": "In this lesson we explore the Islamic legal concept of Daʿwā (الدَّعْوَى), which refers to a claim or lawsuit brought before a court. We examine the roles of the two primary parties involved in litigation: the plaintiff (الْمُدَّعِي – al-muddaʿī) and the defendant (الْمُدَّعَى عَلَيْهِ – al-muddaʿā ʿalayh). The lesson explains that a Daʿwā occurs when a plaintiff makes a claim in court seeking to establish an obligation or right against the defendant. To properly understand this subject, we note that it often requires familiarity with other areas of Islamic jurisprudence, such as marriage (نِكَاح – nikāḥ), divorce (طَلَاق – ṭalāq), and inheritance (مِيرَاث – mīrāth), since legal disputes may arise from these matters.\n\nIn this lesson we also discuss the importance of clearly defining the claim and identifying the item or right being disputed. We learn that the plaintiff must specify the details of the claim and provide evidence or testimony (بَيِّنَة – bayyinah) to support it, while the defendant is required to respond and defend themselves if the claim is brought against them. This structure helps ensure fairness and proper procedure in legal proceedings.\n\nIn this lesson we further examine the procedural requirements of litigation. We explain that a valid claim must clearly state the type and amount involved, whether the matter concerns tangible property or intangible rights. For example, in cases involving real estate, the plaintiff must clearly identify the property and demonstrate that it is currently in the possession of the defendant. We also discuss the importance of precision and transparency in legal claims in order to prevent fraud or conspiracies.\n\nFinally, in this lesson we review additional courtroom procedures such as the role of oaths (يَمِين – yamīn) and the circumstances in which a plaintiff may request that the defendant take an oath. We also highlight the importance of witness testimony in establishing the validity of a claim. Overall, the lesson provides an overview of how Islamic law organizes lawsuits and emphasizes the importance of clarity, evidence, and adherence to legal procedures in resolving disputes.",
