        "overview": "In this video we discuss the signs of khafḏ and jazm. Khafḏ has three signs: kasrah, which is the default, yā, and fatḥah, both of which take its place. Kasrah is used in the singular noun, in the munṣarif broken plural, and in the sound feminine plural. Yā is a sign of khafḏ in the six special nouns, in the dual and what follows its rule, and in the sound masculine plural and its likes. Fatḥah is a sign of khafḏ in the non-munṣarif noun whether singular or broken plural, unless it is made muḍāf or takes alif-lām.\nJazm has two signs: sukūn, which is the default, and deletion, which takes its place. Sukūn marks jazm in the fiʿl muḍāriʿ with a sound ending that has nothing attached to it. Deletion marks jazm in the fiʿl muḍāriʿ ending with a weak letter, and in verbs whose rafʿ is by the nūn, where the nūn is dropped.",
