        "overview": "In this video, we continue our overview of the signs of i’rāb and discuss assumed i’rāb. The noun هن is sometimes included with the asmāʾ sittah, but the preferred usage is to show iʿrāb on its nūn (naqṣ), which is why most grammarians count only five. The amthilah khamsah are verbs with dual, plural, or feminine pronouns attached; they take rafʿ by keeping the nūn and show naṣb and jazm by dropping it.\nAltogether, there are fourteen iʿrāb signs: four original—ḍammah, fatḥah, kasrah, sukūn—and ten substitutes found in seven cases, such as the non-munṣarif, sound feminine plural, weak-ending fiʿl muḍāriʿ, the dual, the sound masculine plural, the five nouns, and the five verbs.\nIʿrāb may be visible or assumed. Assumed iʿrāb occurs when the vowel cannot appear or is too heavy, such as in words attached to yāʾ al-mutakallim, or in nouns ending with alif (maqṣūr) or yāʾ (manqūṣ). In maqṣūr nouns, all signs are assumed; in manqūṣ, the ḍammah and kasrah are assumed, but the fatḥah appears. In verbs ending with alif, wāw, or yāʾ, certain vowels are also assumed, while jazm is shown by deletion.",
