        "overview": "In this lesson we finish our discussion on the ġayr munṣarif ism and go through the different types of isms in terms of definitness. Jam‘ Muntahā al-Jumū‘ is a cause when it has two mutaḥarrik letters, or a sequence of mutaḥarrik–sākin–mutaḥarrik, and is free from ending with a round tā. Alif Maqṣūrah is a cause if the ism follows patterns like: فَعْلى، فُعْلى، فِعْلى، فِعالى، لُعالى. Alif Mamdūdah is a cause in a singular ism when three letters precede the alif, and the hamzah is neither a root letter nor derived from one, such as in أعداء (from أعداو). As for ‘Ujmah, it counts as a cause only if the name was adopted into Arabic at the time of its borrowing; later names remain munṣarif.\nThe ism is of two types: nakirah and ma‘rifah. Nakirah is the default: a general noun referring to its class, like كتاب, رجل, فرس. It is defined as any noun to which alif lām can be added to indicate ta‘rīf, or which comes in the place of a noun that accepts alif lām, such as ذو (in the sense of صاحب). Ma‘rifah has six kinds: pronoun (the most definite, with first person strongest, then second, then third), proper noun, demonstrative, relative pronoun, noun made definite with alif lām, and noun annexed to one of these (sharing its level of definiteness, except annexation to a pronoun which is like a proper noun). The most definite of all is lafẓ al-jalālah by consensus.",
