        "overview": "In this video, we discuss the ṣilah. Every ism mawṣūl needs a ṣilah after it, containing a pronoun that refers back to it. In وكانوا فيه من الزاهدين, الزاهدين acts as the ism mawṣūl, so it’s interpreted as وكانوا زاهدين فيه من الزاهدين where فيه links to the first زاهدين.\nIf the ism mawṣūl is naṣ, the pronoun matches it in gender; if mushtarak, it may refer to the word (singular) or meaning (plural), as in ومن يعص الله ورسوله فإن له نار جهنم خالدين فيها — له refers to من as singular, خالدين as plural.\nThe ṣilah can be a jumlah (verbal or nominal) or a shibh al-jumlah.\nJumlah: جاء الذي قام أبوه or جاء الذي أبوه قائم.\n\nShibh al-jumlah: (1) Ẓarf: جاء الذي عندك, (2) Jār–majrūr: جاء الذي في الدار (with hidden استقر), (3) Derived ṣifah: ism fā‘il or maf‘ūl with ال.\n\nThe ‘āid must match the ism mawṣūl in number and gender. It can be omitted if understood, e.g. أيهم أشد or ما تسرون (from ما تسرونه). It cannot be omitted if essential (جاء الذي إياه أكرمت). If the pronoun is in jarr, omission is allowed when the ḥarf jarr repeats with the same meaning (ويشرب مما تشربون منه), but not if meanings differ (مررت بالذي سررت به).",
