        "overview": "In this video we discuss the fā’il. The word المرفوعات refers to ten types of nouns in the rafʿ state.\n\nThe author begins with the fāʿil because its grammatical agent is stronger than that of the mubtadaʾ and its rafʿ is more crucial for distinguishing it from the mafʿūl.\nThe fāʿil is the marfūʿ noun mentioned after its verb or something in the meaning of a verb, either ẓāhir (explicit or interpreted) or muḍmar (hidden). Examples include قام زيد and أقائم الزيدان.\nRules of the fāʿil:\nIt cannot be omitted as it is an essential element.\n\nIt cannot precede the verb; if it appears to, a hidden pronoun is assumed, and the first noun is treated as a mubtadaʾ or the fāʿil of an omitted verb.\n\nThe verb remains singular even if the fāʿil is dual or plural, though some Arabs used plural/dual endings — a dialect called luġat akalūnī al-barāġīth.\n\nThe verb must be feminine when the fāʿil is a real feminine (قامت هند), optionally when figurative (طلعت الشمس). The Kūfiyyūn consider all plurals feminine, unlike the Baṣriyyūn.\n\nNormally, the fāʿil follows the verb and precedes the mafʿūl. It may come after the mafʿūl for meaning or necessity, or the mafʿūl may precede both for emphasis or when required by an interrogative.",
