        "overview": "In this video, we take a quick look at the manṣūbāt and begin discussing the maf’ūl bihi. The manṣūbāt (nouns that enter the naṣb state) are fifteen: the maf‘ūl bihi, the munādā, the maṣdar (maf‘ūl muṭlaq), ẓarf al-zamān, ẓarf al-makān (maf‘ūl fīhi), the maf‘ūl li-ajlihi, the maf‘ūl ma‘ahu, what resembles the maf‘ūl bihi, the ḥāl, the tamyīz, the khabar of kāna and its sisters, the khabar of the particles that resemble laysa, the khabar of the verbs of near-action, the ism of inna and its sisters, the ism of lā of absolute negation, and whatever follows a manṣūb (four types).\nThe maf‘ūl bihi is the noun upon which the action occurs, such as ضربت زيدًا and اتقوا الله, and it is either visible or hidden. The visible appears in wording, while the hidden may be attached (أكرمني) or detached (إياي). Its default position is after the fā‘il, but it may come before it either optionally or necessarily, and it may even precede both the verb and the subject. Sometimes the verb governing it is omitted, either optionally (قالوا خيرًا) or necessarily in specific cases such as al-istiġāl (زيدًا ضربته), the munādā (يا زيد), al-ikhtiṣāṣ (نحن العربَ), al-iġrā (الصلاةَ الصلاةَ), al-taḥḍīr (إياك الأسدَ), proverbs (الكلابَ على البقر), and expressions treated like proverbs (أهلًا وسهلًا).",
