        "portalId": "390fa765-fdad-4cce-846d-4dd73f5ed0e3",
        "title": "Lesson 41 : The Maf’ūl Fīhi, Maf’ūl li Ajlihi, and Maf’ūl Ma’ahu",
        "overview": "In this video we discuss the maf’ūl fīhi and the maf’ūl ma’ahu. The word al-maf‘ūl fīhi refers to an adverbial noun of time or place (ẓarf al-zamān and ẓarf al-makān). A noun is only a ẓarf when it expresses the time or place in which the action happens. If the meaning shows the action is directed toward the word, not inside it, it becomes a maf‘ūl bih, like خفتُ يومَ القيامة and the verse يخافون يوماً—the fear is of the Day itself, not within a time span.\nA proper ẓarf indicates when the action occurs, like خفتُ اليوم. Time-words may be indefinite, measured, or specific, and all can function as ẓarf with an implied في. Examples include اليوم, الليلة, غدوة, بكرة, سحراً, غداً, عتمة, صباحاً, مساءً, حيناً, عاماً, شهراً, أسبوعاً, ساعةً. Their meanings include: غدوة (between dawn and sunrise), بكرة (early morning), السحر (just before dawn), عتمة (first third of the night), غداً (the next day), الصبح (early light).\nFor place, only three types may act as ẓarf: mubham directions like فوق and أمام; measured distances like ميل and كيلومتر; and nouns of place derived from a maṣdar such as مجلس. Other place-words require a preposition, except in cases where the preposition is treated as omitted, as in دخلت المسجد.\nThe maf‘ūl min ajlihi (maf‘ūl lahu) is a manṣūb noun explaining the reason for an action, like قام زيد إجلالاً لعمرو. It must be a maṣdar, share the same time as the action, and have the same doer. If these conditions fail, a lām is required, such as تأهبتُ للسفر.\nThe maf‘ūl ma‘ahu is a manṣūb noun after a wāw meaning “with,” expressing accompaniment: جاء الأميرُ والجيشَ, استوى الماءُ والخشبةَ, أنا سائرٌ والنيلَ. It must be a noun and must follow this special wāw. Sometimes only naṣb is correct, sometimes only ʿaṭf, and sometimes both are possible depending on the meaning.",
