        "title": "Lesson 42 : The Ḥāl",
        "overview": "In this video, we continue our discussion on the maf’ūl ma’ahu and begin discussing the ḥāl. Sometimes the maf‘ūl ma‘ahu must be manṣūb, like the two examples mentioned earlier, and in لا تنهَ عن القبيح وإتيانَه because the prohibition concerns combining both acts, not ordinary coordination. The same applies to مات زيد وطلوعَ الشمس. In the verse فَأَجْمِعوا أمرَكم وشركاءَكم (with the reading فأَجمعوا), ‘aṭf is impossible since أجمع applies to non-physical matters, while شركاءكم is physical.\nAt times, maf‘ūl ma‘ahu is preferred, like قمتُ وزيداً, and at other times, ‘aṭf is better, like جاء زيد وعمرو, which is the more common form.\nThe ḥāl is a manṣūb word (or a sentence functioning like one) that clarifies the state of the subject or object. It can be from the doer—جاء زيد راكباً—from the object—ركبتُ الفرسَ مُسَرَّجاً—or from both—لقيتُ عبدَ الله راكبين. It may also come from a majrūr tied to the action, as in مررتُ بهند ماشية. It can even relate to something linked by iḍāfah when the muḏāf acts on its subject—إليه مرجعُكم جميعاً—or when it resembles a part of the possessed noun—فاتبعوا ملةَ إبراهيم حنيفاً.\nThe ḥāl must be indefinite; if it appears definite, it is interpreted as indefinite, like جاء زيدٌ وحدَه. It is usually a derived adjective, but a fixed noun may be used when carrying a derived meaning, such as resemblance (بدت الجارية قمراً), mutual action (يداً بيد), or sequence (ادخلوا رجلاً رجلاً).",
