        "overview": "Tamyīz is the manṣūb noun that removes vagueness—either from an essence (ḍāt) or an attribution (nisbah).\nTamyīz al-Ḍāt\nThis clarifies something vague in essence, and it appears after four types:\n- Numbers — like اشتريتُ عشرين غلامًا and ملكتُ تسعين نعجةً.\n- Measurements — weight, volume, distance, etc., like اشتريتُ قفيزًا بُرًّا and منًّا سمنًا. You may also say من برٍّ; this flexibility is unique to measurements.\n- What resembles a measurement — like مثقال ذرةٍ خيرًا.\n- A part of what is clarified — like هذا خاتمٌ حديدًا.\nNote that the number tamyīz may be:\nKinā’ī, used with كم (istifhāmiyyah), and can be majrūr after a preposition.\nor Ṣarīḥ, especially from 11–99 (others are muḏāf to the tamyīz).\n\nTamyīz al-Nisbah (Tamyīz al-Jumlah)\nThis clarifies a vague attribution and is either transformed (muḥawwal) or not transformed.",
