        "title": "Lesson 46 : The Ḥurūf Al-Jarr",
        "overview": "In this video we discuss the ḥurūf al-jarr. The major ḥurūf al-jarr are: من، إلى، عن، على، في، الباء، الكاف، اللام، حتى، الواو، التاء، رب، مذ، منذ.\nThe first seven enter both on nouns and pronouns—like {ومِنك ومِن نوحٍ}, {إليه مرجعكم}. Their primary meanings are: min for initiation, ilā for the endpoint, ʿan for distancing, ʿalā for ‘upon,’ fī for containment, bāʾ for attachment or cause, and lām for exclusivity or ownership. A mafʿūl bihi can be explicit (قتلتَ زيدًا) or non-explicit, expressed through a jār–majrūr depending on the meaning of its ḥarf.\nThe remaining ḥurūf enter only on nouns—not pronouns—except in poetic necessity. Some of them, like ك، حتى، and the general wāw, come before any visible noun, while others are restricted: رب usually enters upon nakirah and may also attach to a hidden pronoun with a tamyīz after it (ربّه فتيّةً). رب may also be omitted, leaving its jarr after a wāw, and it can appear after ف frequently, after بل rarely, and sometimes without any preceding particle.\nمذ and منذ are specific to time: منذ indicates the starting point, while مذ can express the entire duration.\nAn extra mā may appear after من، عن، and ب without blocking their jarr, as in {مما خطيئاتهم}. It may also come after ك and رب, often stopping their ‘amal so they enter upon sentences, as in poetry, though sometimes the jarr remains.\nSome ḥurūf are restricted to lafẓ al-jalālah or to رب with special forms (تالله، تربِّ الكعبة). Certain forms like تالرحمن are rare.",
