        "title": "Lesson 47 : Iḏāfah",
        "overview": "In this video, we discuss iḏāfah. المخفوض بالإضافة refers to a noun made majrūr because it is the muḍāf ilayh, and the muḍāf is the actual ʿāmil—example: غلامُ زيدٍ. The muḍāf must lose tanwīn (and the dual/plural nūn) because iḍāfah already gives taʿrīf or takhṣīṣ, and this is why it normally cannot take an alif lām.\nIḍāfah is understood in three ways:\n– With a ل, which is the most common (غلام زيد).\n– With من, when the second noun is a part of the first (خاتم حديد).\n– With في, when the action of the first occurs within the second (مكر الليل).\nThese are conceptual only—not meant to be inserted in speech.\nIḍāfah is either maʿnawiyyah, which gives taʿrīf/takhṣīṣ (like غلامُ زيدٍ), or lafẓiyyah, where the muḍāf is a ṣifah, and the muḍāf ilayh is its maʿmūl (like ضاربُ زيدٍ). Lafẓiyyah doesn’t give a definition or specification; it only lightens pronunciation.\nThe stronger view is that the muḍāf ilayh is majrūr by the muḍāf itself, shown by how pronouns attach: غلامه. The majrūr tābiʿ will be discussed later under the tawābiʿ.",
