        "portalId": "855ab2fd-6f9e-4a08-9919-c216e7b4aa84",
        "title": "Lesson 49 : The Givers of Jazm",
        "overview": "The jawāzim are eighteen tools that give jazm to the fiʿl al-muḍāriʿ. Ibn Mālik lists: the word لا for prohibition or duʿā; the word لام of command/duʿā; the word لم; the word لما; and eleven conditional tools: إن، من، ما، مهما، أيّ، متى، أيان، أين، إذما، حيثما، أنّى.\nلا and the lām of command give jazm when expressing prohibition or supplication. لم and لما negate the past and give jazm to one verb; لما’s negation extends to the present and implies expectation.\nThe conditional particles fall into three groups: those attached to ما (مثل حيثما، إذما), those that never attach like مهما أنى, and those that accept both forms like إن أي مت). Forms like إمّا and أينما come from this.\nJawāzim are of two types: those that give jazm to one verb (مثل لم، لما، لام الأمر، ولا الناهية), and those that give jazm to two verbs—the verb of the condition and its answer. The eleven conditional tools do this; all are nouns except إن and إذما.\nIf the jawāb cannot serve as a conditional verb—such as a nominal sentence, a request, a fiʿl jāmid, or a verb with قد، ما، لم، لن، س، سوف—it must be preceded by فاء. Sometimes إذا الفجائية enters the jawāب. Rare poetic usage shows إذا giving jazm.\nIf the conditional particle is a ظرف, it is منصوب by the conditional verb, except إذا, which is منصوب by its jawab. Some tools take the meaning of a mafʿūl muṭlaq, like مهما تُكرم أكرم.\nWhen both sharṭ and jawāb are muḍāriʿ—or the sharṭ is muḍāriʿ, and the jawāب is māḍī without فاء—the jawāb must be majzūm (وإن تعودوا نعدْ). If the sharṭ is māḍī and the jawāb is muḍāriʿ, the muḍāriʿ may be in rafʿ or jazm (من كان يريد… نوفِّ).",
