        "overview": "In this video we discuss ‘aṭf. There two kinds of ‘aṭf: ‘aṭf al-bayān and ‘aṭf al-nasaq. ‘Aṭf al-bayān functions like the badal and is used to clarify or specify the word before it. It is usually a jāmid word that follows its matbū‘ in i‘rāb, gender, number, and definiteness. Scholars note that it can appear with both ma‘rifah and nakirah nouns, and it often comes after a kunyah, laqab, or ism ishārah. In many cases it can be analyzed as badal, though certain structures prevent that interpretation.\n\n‘Aṭf al-nasaq connects two elements through one of ten particles such as wāw, fā’, thumma, aw, bal, and lakin. The ma‘ṭūf always matches the ma‘ṭūf ‘alayh in i‘rāb, while each particle adds a specific meaning: wāw for simple joining, fā’ for sequence with closeness, thumma for sequence with delay, and others for contrast, choice, or negation.",
