        "title": "Lesson 53 : Tawkīd Ma’nawī and Badal",
        "overview": "In this video, we discuss tawkīd ma‘nawī—its specific words, how they are used, and the grammatical rules that govern them. The words can be divided into two groups: those that must attach to a matching pronoun (نفس، عين، جميع، كلا، كلتا) and those that do not (أجمع، جمعاء، أجمعون، جُمَع). The words نفس and عين change according to singular, dual, and plural, while كل، جميع، and عامة emphasize singulars and plurals but never duals. كلا and كلتا are specifically for the dual.\nEmphasis can be strengthened by adding forms like أجمع، جمعاء، أجمعون، جُمَع, with Qur’anic examples, and notes rare forms such as أكتع، أبصع، أبتع. Tawkīd always follows the emphasized word in grammatical state and definiteness. Basri grammarians disallow emphasizing a nakirah, but Kufis allow it when beneficial.\nBadal is a substitute that repeats a meaning while adding clarity. It’s of four types —kull min al-kull, ba‘ḍ min al-kull, ishtimāl, and mubāyin—with examples from the Qur’an and grammar rules such as the requirement of a returning pronoun in some forms. A verb may be substituted for a verb, that badal can occur from nakirah to ma‘rifah in certain cases, and that an entire sentence can be replaced by another when the second is clearer.",
