        "title": "Lesson 54 : The Asmā That Govern Like a Fi’l",
        "overview": "In this video, we discuss asmā that have ‘amal even though the default for ‘amal belongs to verbs. Seven types of nouns have this capacity, beginning with the verbal noun (maṣdar). The maṣdar works like a verb when its meaning can be understood with the word \"أن\" or \"ما\" and a verb, though Ibn Mālik and Ibn ʿAqīl differ on how strict this condition is. It may govern directly or indirectly through reinterpretation, but it won’t work when that meaning cannot be implied.\nThe maṣdar appears in three forms—muḍāf, munawwan (tanwīn), and with alif-lām—with the first two commonly showing ‘amal and the third rarely. Several restrictions limit where the maṣdar can govern, including not being diminutive, not separated from its maʿmūl, and not occurring after it.\nThe second type that works like a verb is the ism al-fāʿil. With alif-lām, it governs without conditions. Without alif-lām, it governs only when referring to the present or future and when supported by a negation, question, mubtada, mawṣūf, or context of ḥāl.\nThe third type is the patterns of hyperbole (faʿʿāl, faʿūl, mifʿāl, faʿīl, faʿil). These behave like the ism al-fāʿil: with alif-lām they govern unrestrictedly; without alif-lām they follow the same conditions required of the ism al-fāʿil.",
