        "overview": "In this video, we discuss the fourth and fifth types of nouns that perform the actions of verbs. The fourth is the ism al-mafʿūl, which governs like the passive verb, producing a nāʾib al-fāʿil rather than a fāʿil. Its conditions match those of the ism al-fāʿil: if supported (or defined by alif-lām), it can govern.\n\nThe fifth is the ṣifah mushabbahah (e.g., ḥasan, ẓarīf), which indicates a permanent trait. It may govern one word and appears in three common structural patterns: raising the noun as fāʿil, lowering it by resemblance to the mafʿūl, or using tamyīz; it can also come through iḍāfah. Its governing ability is weak, requiring proximity to a pronoun linked to the described noun, and certain grammatical conditions determine whether the governed term appears in rafʿ, naṣb, or jarr.",
