        "overview": "In this video, we explain the scenarios of the ṣifah mushabbahah and its maʿmūl. The governed noun can appear in three grammatical states (rafʿ, jarr, naṣb), producing three core patterns like: زيد حسنُ وجهُه، زيد حسنُ الوجهِ، زيد حسن وجهًا. Since the ṣifah and its maʿmūl may each be either maʿrifah or nakirah, and the maʿmūl itself has six structural forms, the combinations reach thirty-six possibilities. Four forms are invalid because they treat a descriptive iḍāfah as if it were real (iḍāfah lafẓiyyah), giving the ṣifah definiteness while the maʿmūl lacks it. The rest vary in strength, preference, or weakness.\nThe text then moves to ism al-tafḍīl (like أكرم and أفضل). It compares two entities but may also be used without an actual comparison (الله أكبر). It doesn’t normally govern a mafʿūl bihi and doesn’t show an apparent fāʿil, except in the special construction known as mas’alt al-kuḥl, where awsaʿ structure and denial allow a rare raising. Ism al-tafḍīl also takes tamyīz, and it can govern in jār–majrūr and in ẓarf expressions like زيد أفضلُ منك اليومَ.",
