        "overview": "In Arabic grammar (النحو al-naḥw), nouns (الأسماء al-asmāʾ) are generally expected to exhibit inflection (الإعراب al-iʿrāb), reflecting their grammatical roles within a sentence. This is due to their ability to carry multiple syntactic functions, such as subject (فاعل fāʿil), object (مفعول mafʿūl), or predicate (خبر khabar). This variability necessitates visible grammatical markers.\nHowever, a subset of nouns remains indeclinable (مبني mabnī), meaning they retain a fixed form regardless of their syntactic position. This raises an important theoretical question: Why do certain nouns remain mabnī despite possessing the characteristics that normally require iʿrāb?\nThis lesson explores the linguistic principles behind this phenomenon, focusing on the concept of resemblance (الشبه al-shabah) between nouns and particles (الحروف al-ḥurūf), which serves as the primary explanation for mabnī forms.",
