        "overview": "The study of Arabic grammar (النحو al-naḥw) often reveals subtle yet profound discussions surrounding seemingly simple words. One such word is أَمْسِ (amsi), meaning yesterday. Despite its apparent simplicity, classical grammarians differed regarding its grammatical behavior—whether it is مَبْنِيّ (mabnī), meaning indeclinable, or مُعْرَب (muʿrab), meaning declinable.\nThis chapter explores the grammatical rulings of أَمْسِ, the conditions under which it becomes مَبْنِيّ عَلَى الْكَسْرِ (mabnī ʿalā al-kasr), the اختلاف (ikhtilāf, scholarly disagreement) between the grammarians of the Ḥijāz and Banū Tamīm, and the underlying grammatical principles that inform these positions.",
