        "overview": "In this lesson, the discussion moves from the chapter of nouns (asmāʾ) to the chapter of verbs (afʿāl) in the famous grammatical text Qaṭr al-Nadā wa Ball al-Ṣadā by Ibn Hishām al-Anṣārī. The author of the text does not attempt to mention every possible grammatical discussion related to nouns and verbs. Rather, he focuses on the most important foundational principles that students require in order to understand Arabic grammar systematically.\nThe earlier discussion on nouns concentrated upon two central themes: the identifying signs (ʿalāmāt) of nouns and the grammatical states of declension (iʿrāb) and indeclinability (bināʾ). In a similar manner, the discussion regarding verbs focuses upon the divisions of the verb, their identifying signs, and their grammatical rulings.\nThe lesson also transitions into a deeper theoretical discussion concerning the definition of the verb (fiʿl) according to the scholars of Arabic grammar (nuḥāt). This leads to a broader explanation of semantic indication (dalālah), particularly the famous logical divisions of indication: muṭābaqah, taḍammun, and iltizām.\nThe lesson therefore combines practical grammar with foundational linguistic theory.",
