        "overview": "One of the important discussions in Arabic grammar (Naḥw النَّحْو) concerns a small group of words over which the early grammarians disagreed regarding their grammatical classification. The majority of scholars maintained that these words are genuine verbs (afʿāl أَفْعَال), whereas a minority argued that they are particles (ḥurūf حُرُوف) that merely resemble verbs. Among the most significant of these disputed words are لَيْسَ (laysa) and عَسَى (ʿasā).\nThis discussion is not merely theoretical. Whether these words are considered verbs or particles directly affects their grammatical analysis (iʿrāb إِعْرَاب), the interpretation of Qur'ānic verses, classical Arabic poetry, and the principles governing Arabic syntax. Throughout the history of Arabic grammar, the Basran (Baṣriyyūn البَصْرِيُّون) and Kufan (Kūfiyyūn الكُوفِيُّون) schools developed different approaches to this issue, producing one of the most detailed debates in the science of Naḥw.\nThe lesson examines the arguments presented by both sides, evaluates their evidences, and explains why the majority of grammarians ultimately regarded both لَيْسَ and عَسَى as genuine verbs.",
