    "description": "This course explores Jalāl al-Dīn al-Maḥallī's commentary on Imām Juwaynī's foundational text in Islamic legal theory, offering valuable insights into Uṣūl al-fiqh. It examines the evolution of legal thought in Islamic tradition, bridging theory with practical application through real-life case studies and examples. The course aims to make Islamic legal theory more engaging and accessible, deepening students' understanding of how these principles are applied in various contexts while enhancing retention of key concepts.",
    "courseIntroduction": "• The course will begin with a brief introduction to the discipline of Uṣūl al-fiqh, followed by an application based study of Al-Maḥallī's commentary.\n• It aims to examine the interplay between al-Maḥallī's commentary and Juwaynī's original work, highlighting the evolution of legal thought in Islamic tradition.\n• The course will place significant emphasis on practical examples to illustrate key concepts, effectively bridging the gap between theoretical understanding and real-world application. By showcasing how legal theory operates in various contexts, students will gain a more dynamic and engaging perspective on this often-overlooked discipline.\n• 1. Practical Focus: The course will highlight real-life scenarios and case studies that demonstrate the principles of Uṣūl al-fiqh, helping to contextualize theoretical concepts.\n• Engagement with Content: By using examples, the course aims to transform the perception of legal theory from a dry, abstract field into a vibrant and relevant study.\n• 2. Deepening Understanding: Students will learn how theoretical frameworks are applied in different situations, leading to a richer comprehension of the nuances and complexities within Islamic jurisprudence.\n• 3. Enhancing Retention: Engaging with examples will not only facilitate better understanding but also aid in retaining knowledge by illustrating the practical implications of legal theories.\n• Through this approach, the course seeks to foster a deeper appreciation for the relevance and importance of legal theory in everyday life and Islamic scholarship.",
