        "portalId": "61d03ff6-1933-41b2-8acd-1810480693fb",
        "title": "Lesson 10: Presentations",
        "overview": "This lesson examines the early documentation (Tadwīn) of ḥadīth and responds to claims—particularly orientalist arguments—that prophetic traditions were not written down until a late period. The discussion centers around Chapter 3 and Chapter 4 of Dr. Muṣṭafā al-Aʿẓamī’s research, in which he analyzes:\nWhy some scholars (both Muslim and non-Muslim) believed ḥadīth were written late,\nThe role of memory versus writing in early Islamic society,\nThe issue of the Prophetic prohibition of writing,\nConcrete historical evidence of writing among the Ṣaḥābah and Tābiʿūn, and\nThe beginnings of official and systematic Tadwīn in the first and second centuries.\nA central objective of the lesson is methodological: how to evaluate claims critically without conceding false premises, especially those rooted in modern skepticism rather than historical reality.",
