        "overview": "In the study of Uṣūl al-Fiqh, one of the most important subjects is understanding how language conveys legal rulings. The jurist (uṣūlī) does not merely study the eloquence or literary beauty of the Qur’ān and Sunnah. Rather, the primary concern of the uṣūlī is determining how textual expressions produce legal consequences and rulings.\nEarlier discussions introduced the concepts of Al-Khāṣṣ (الخَاصّ) and Al-ʿĀmm (العَامّ). A khāṣṣ expression is a word or phrase that refers to a specific individual, group, or quantity, while an ʿāmm expression refers to a broader class or category. Since such expressions appear throughout the Qur’ān and Sunnah, understanding their legal implications is essential for deriving Islamic rulings.\nThe present discussion focuses on the legal status (ḥukm) of the khāṣṣ expression and how it should be understood when interpreting revelation.",
