        "overview": "One of the central objectives of Uṣūl al-Fiqh (uṣūl al-fiqh) is to establish principles that enable jurists to understand and reconcile the evidences of the Sharīʿah without attributing contradiction to divine revelation. This lesson continues the discussion on the ruling of al-khāṣ (al-khāṣṣ), building upon the previously established principle that a specific expression conveys its intended meaning with certainty unless a valid indicator exists to divert it.\nThe lesson expands this principle beyond specific expressions and demonstrates that it applies to all words possessing a clear and apparent meaning. It also explains how the Hanafi school approaches apparently conflicting evidences, the distinction between clarification and abrogation, and the methodology used when reconciling definitive and speculative proofs. Finally, the lesson provides historical context for the methodological differences between the Hanafi and Shāfiʿī schools regarding the authority of solitary reports (khabar al-wāḥid).",
