        "title": "Lesson 20 | The Minimum Mahr in Islamic Law",
        "overview": "Among the principles discussed by the Hanafi jurists is the rule concerning a specific Qur'ānic text (الْخَاصُّ – al-Khāṣ). They maintain that whenever the Qur'ān establishes a ruling through wording that is specific and decisive, that ruling cannot be overturned by speculative evidence such as an isolated narration (خَبَرُ الْوَاحِدِ – Khabar al-Wāḥid) or analogical reasoning (الْقِيَاسُ – al-Qiyās). Instead, every effort must first be made to reconcile the evidences. Only when reconciliation is impossible is preference given to the stronger proof.\nThe lesson explores this principle through an important juristic discussion concerning the minimum amount of mahr (أَقَلُّ الْمَهْرِ – aqall al-mahr). Although the issue appears to be a practical legal question related to marriage, the real objective is to demonstrate how principles of Uṣūl al-Fiqh govern the interpretation of revealed texts and determine the relationship between Qur'ānic evidence and juristic reasoning.\nRather than debating the amount of mahr itself, the discussion investigates whether the Qur'ān indicates that the Sharīʿah has fixed a minimum amount at all. This question becomes a practical illustration of the Hanafi principle regarding the authority of specific Qur'ānic wording.",
