        "overview": "The lesson examines an important example from the science of uṣūl al-fiqh concerning the meaning and legal force of a general expression, or ʿāmm (عَامّ). The discussion centers on the statement of Allah Most High:\nوَلَا تَأْكُلُوا مِمَّا لَمْ يُذْكَرِ اسْمُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْهِ ۖ وَإِنَّهُ لَفِسْقٌ ۗ وَإِنَّ الشَّيَاطِينَ لَيُوحُونَ إِلَىٰ أَوْلِيَائِهِمْ لِيُجَادِلُوكُمْ ۖ وَإِنْ أَطَعْتُمُوهُمْ إِنَّكُمْ لَمُشْرِكُونَ\n“Do not eat from that over which the name of Allah has not been mentioned. Indeed, it is sinful; and indeed, the devils inspire their allies to argue with you. If you obey them, you would indeed be associators.”\n— Qurʾān 6:121\nThe central usūlī question is how broadly the expression مِمَّا لَمْ يُذْكَرِ اسْمُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْهِ should be understood, and how it relates to the established juristic difference concerning an animal slaughtered by a Muslim without mentioning Allah’s name.\nThe lesson uses this example to illuminate a broader principle: a general expression may appear to include many individual cases, but the jurist must determine precisely which cases actually fall within its intended scope. This requires attention not only to the wording of the text, but also to other evidence, the context of revelation, the distinction between specification and abrogation, and the principles governing conflicting proofs.\nThe discussion is particularly important because it illustrates the Hanafi usūlī distinction between الْعَامُّ الَّذِي أُرِيدَ بِهِ الْخُصُوصُ (al-ʿāmm alladhī urīda bihi al-khuṣūṣ), a general expression whose intended referents were restricted from the outset, and الْعَامُّ الَّذِي خُصَّ عَنْهُ الْبَعْضُ (al-ʿāmm alladhī khuṣṣa ʿanhu al-baʿḍ), a general expression from which some cases were subsequently specified.\nThe lesson also demonstrates an important methodological principle. A scholar may use an example not merely to establish the immediate ruling under discussion but also to indicate another usūlī debate that will be treated later. In this case, the example of slaughter illustrates the limits of specification and the relationship between a general Qurʾānic text and a later or independent report.",
