        "overview": "The present discussion continues the study of al-Muṭlaq (الْمُطْلَق), the unrestricted expression, and al-Muqayyad (الْمُقَيَّد), the restricted or qualified expression. It addresses an important question that arises from the distinction between nafy (النَّفْي), negation, and ithbāt (الإِثْبَات), affirmation. It then introduces the closely related principle of Ziyādah ʿalā al-Naṣṣ (زِيَادَةٌ عَلَى النَّصِّ), meaning the addition of a legally effective requirement to an existing text.\n\nThe lesson explains that an apparently simple distinction between an unrestricted and a restricted statement can produce major methodological consequences. The central issue is whether the second text merely clarifies the first text, known as bayān (بَيَان), or whether it changes the legal implication of the first text. In the Ḥanafī approach presented in the lesson, an addition that changes the conditions necessary for valid fulfillment may be treated as naskh (نَسْخ), or legal abrogation or modification, rather than mere clarification.\n\nThis discussion is especially significant because it affects the relationship between Qur’ānic texts, Sunnah reports, khabar al-wāḥid (خَبَرُ الْوَاحِد), solitary reports, and qiyās (قِيَاس), analogical reasoning. It also provides the methodological basis for later legal discussions concerning the conditions of acts such as wuḍūʾ (وُضُوء)",
