        "overview": "The second part of the lesson continues the discussion of additional conditions imposed upon the Qurʾānic description of wuḍūʾ. The specific focus here is الْمُوَالَاةُ (al-muwālāt), continuity between the acts of wuḍūʾ, followed by التَّسْمِيَةُ (al-tasmiyah), pronouncing the name of Allah at the beginning of wuḍūʾ.\nThe instructor defines muwālāt as performing the acts of wuḍūʾ continuously, such that the next limb is washed before the previous limb dries under normal conditions. In other words, the acts are performed one after another without an extended interruption.\nAccording to the Mālikī school, muwālāt is treated as a condition in an important strand of the madhhab. The lesson then examines the evidences offered for this position and evaluates them from the Ḥanafī uṣūlī perspective.",
